E. REPAIR REPORT/DOSSIER EXPORTS: REAL, COMPLETE, READABLE FILES
The PDF, JSON, and other download actions on the last page currently produce tiny placeholder outputs. First read the existing handlers and report why they produce placeholders. Then repair every export format already shown in the UI. Do not add new formats.

E0. Architecture: one source of truth.
- Find where the active case's state lives (store, context, props) and which selectors exist. Identify any data the report component cannot reach.
- Create or reuse ONE pure function, for example buildReportModel(caseState), that returns the structured report. Every existing export format must be generated from this model so PDF, JSON, and CSV/other cannot disagree.
- Do not use a fixed/hardcoded report, generic filler lines, fake backend responses, an LLM call, or any network request. Generate the summary with deterministic templates and build everything locally in the browser. Do not log full report contents to the console and do not cache report data in browser storage (it contains personal data).
- Absent fields are omitted or shown as "Not recorded". Never invent evidence, relationships, amounts, or verification results. Label demo-seeded data as demo/sample case data in the metadata or a concise note.
- Make the model robust to empty or partial cases (no evidence, no timeline, missing dates). It must produce a valid report with "Not recorded" rather than throw.
- Do not make legal claims (admissibility, "court-ready", authenticity). If a report status field does not exist, use "Draft: generated from application state".

E1. Report content. Include these sections, in order, wherever data exists:
 1. Title, case ID/name, generated date/time with explicit timezone (show the local timezone and the UTC ISO time), and report status.
 2. Executive summary in plain language: what happened, principal risk, known exposure, and the conclusion the recorded data supports.
 3. Case overview and key figures from the selected case.
 4. Chronological timeline: date/time, event, involved people/accounts, amount where present, linked evidence, warning/status. Include user-added or edited steps in the current state. Sort chronologically with a stable sort. Put items without a date at the end as "Date not recorded". Show timestamps as recorded; if a source timestamp has no timezone, say so and do not silently convert it.
 5. Evidence register: artifact ID, filename, type, size, file fingerprint/hash, acquisition/source, verification status when recorded.
 6. People, phone numbers, accounts, wallets, URLs/domains, IPs, and other correlated entities, with relationship descriptions supported by the current graph.
 7. Financial movements/exposure summary derived from the records. Deduplicate only when a real transaction ID supports it. If there is no stable identifier, list the records separately and flag "possible duplicates unresolved". Never sum different currencies together; give separate subtotals per currency.
 8. Warnings/anomalies, including photo timestamp/EXIF discrepancies when present, each with a one-line plain-English explanation of why it matters.
 9. Integrity/chain-of-custody results with their exact scope. Distinguish computed verification from demo-presentation states. The homepage "Fingerprint verified" animation is presentation-only and must never be cited as proof of integrity.
10. Conclusion/next review points and a short glossary of the essential technical terms that remain.
Use plain language. On first use write "file fingerprint (hash)" and explain specialist terms briefly. Avoid jargon-heavy boilerplate and long generic disclaimers.

E2. PDF generation.
- Inspect whether a working PDF library/utility already exists, and reuse it first.
- If none exists, add at most ONE established PDF-generation package (such as pdf-lib or jsPDF), loaded with a dynamic import() so it stays out of the main bundle. Add no table plugin and no second package. Implement tables and text wrapping with a small internal layout helper. Report the package name and version. If a usable library is already installed, add nothing.
- If installation is blocked, use the best browser-native alternative only if it truly produces a PDF, and mark the requirement Partial. Never present HTML or text renamed to .pdf as a PDF.
- Output must be a valid PDF (begins with %PDF), not a placeholder, a 2–4 line summary, or a screenshot of the UI.
- Layout: headings, spacing, wrapped text, page breaks, and a header/footer with case ID and "Page X of Y" where the chosen library supports it. Repeat table headers across pages. Avoid orphaned headings and blank pages.
- Long filenames, account IDs, URLs, descriptions, and hashes wrap or break at character boundaries and never clip or overflow. Use a monospace font for hashes.
- Non-Latin text (for example Devanagari or accented letters, where such data exists): check whether the PDF font renders it. Embedding a custom font often needs a second package, so do NOT add one. Use a font file already in the project's assets only if one exists. Otherwise use a documented safe substitution (for example a visible placeholder such as "?" or transliteration) and flag the limitation in the report. Never silently emit garbled glyphs. Do not load fonts from a CDN.
- Filename: selected case ID/name plus a date, sanitized for punctuation, whitespace, and length.

E3. JSON and other existing formats.
- JSON: valid UTF-8, a structured object (not a quoted string), derived from the same report model. Sections such as case, summary, metrics, timeline, evidence, entities, relationships, financialSummary, warnings, integrity, and metadata (schemaVersion, generatedAt, timezone). Keep complete hashes and URLs intact.
- CSV or any other format already in the UI: complete records with headers and correct escaping (RFC 4180 for CSV). For spreadsheet-facing text cells, neutralize formula injection (cells starting with =, +, -, @) without altering real numeric values or the underlying data. A UTF-8 BOM is allowed for Excel.
- Each action downloads the correct format with the correct MIME type and a sanitized filename. Create the Blob/object URL on demand, and revoke it after a short delay (about 1–4 s) rather than immediately after click(), which can cancel downloads in some browsers. Do not block the UI during generation: yield one frame so a loading state paints, disable the button while generating, and ignore repeated clicks until it finishes.
- Derive totals from current records, not from fixed numbers. Avoid double-counting the same transaction across the timeline and entity lists.

E4. Verify exports rather than trusting the code.
- PDF: programmatically check that the first bytes are %PDF, the page count, and that the size is consistent with the report content. Spot-check that a long hash and a long filename are not truncated. Extract text if a tool allows. If the PDF cannot be opened or rendered here, mark the visual check "Not verified".
- JSON: JSON.parse round-trip succeeds, and the counts of timeline steps, evidence files, and entities equal what the UI shows for that case.
- Preview may block downloads (sandboxed iframes often do). If the actual file download cannot be observed, verify the generated Blob in code (size, header bytes, parse) and report the download itself as "Not verified". Do not claim a download worked.
- Test two different existing cases and confirm the files differ and match their cases. If an existing supported edit is available, make one small in-memory edit (such as a timeline detail), confirm the next export reflects it, then undo it. Do not persist test edits or change seed data.
- Also test a case with missing or empty data, if one exists, to confirm the export does not crash.
Acceptance: the PDF action downloads a real, readable, multi-section report. JSON and any other format hold complete current-case data. Switching cases changes the output. No data is invented.

F. MICROINTERACTIONS AND SMOOTH STAGE TRANSITIONS
Keep motion restrained and fast: a precise forensic workbench, not a showy landing page.

F1. Buttons and controls.
- Extend the existing shared button class/component. Do not add a global button:hover rule that could hit stepper pills, dropdown items, graph controls, or canvas controls. Include chips only if they are genuinely interactive.
- Hover (only inside @media (hover: hover) and (pointer: fine)): lift 1–2 px with a subtle shadow/reflection increase.
- Press: scale to about 0.98–0.99, then a short spring-like recovery.
- Focus: a clear :focus-visible ring with no rectangular clipping artifacts.
- Disabled/loading: visibly distinct, with no hover or press animation, covering both :disabled and [aria-disabled="true"].
- Use existing tokens and transform/opacity/box-shadow transitions of about 150–220 ms. No idle animation, exaggerated scaling, or animated backdrop-filter.
- Do not apply a transform to any element that contains fixed-position children or portaled popovers (a transform creates a containing block and breaks fixed descendants).
- Under prefers-reduced-motion: no nonessential movement, only brief state/opacity changes.

F2. Stage transitions.
- Stage state and navigation change IMMEDIATELY on click. Do not delay navigation for an outgoing animation, and do not mount intermediate stages.
- Apply a short incoming fade/slide (about 180–240 ms, at most 4–6 px vertical) to the stage content via a keyed wrapper or scoped CSS animation. If the outgoing content also fades, crossfade within the same short window.
- The workspace shell and background stay stable, with no white or blank flash.
- Do not remount the shared header, stepper, globe, graph, or stateful controls unnecessarily. Preserve existing state behavior. Clean up timers/animation frames on unmount. Add no transition framework or package.
- When the stage changes, scroll the element that actually owns scrolling (the window or an inner workspace scroller, whichever it is) to the top of the destination stage, just below the sticky header. Do not scroll on initial load or when the stage did not change.
- No animation when the already-active stage is clicked. Preserve browser back/forward and existing deep-link behavior. Keyboard-initiated stage changes behave the same as clicks.

F3. Flowing indicator along the stepper connectors.
- Reuse the existing stepper DOM, layout, and connector elements. Do not change the labels, counts, or six-stage navigation. If the connectors are pure CSS with no addressable element, add a single absolutely positioned, pointer-events: none overlay track inside the stepper. It must not change the stepper's size or layout.
- Adjacent move (for example 2 → 3): a restrained blue pulse travels along that connector, then settles at the destination.
- Nonadjacent jump (for example 1 → 5): one continuous directional pulse crosses the intervening connector path. Intermediate stages must NOT become active, mount, load, or execute; only the requested destination loads, once. Forward and backward moves travel in the correct direction.
- Total pulse duration about 250–380 ms regardless of distance. No fixed per-stage delay.
- If the user selects another stage mid-animation, cancel/replace the old animation and navigate to the latest target immediately.
- The effect is visual-only. It must not change routing, active-stage state, callbacks, loading, case state, or data. Active-stage semantics (including aria-current) must stay correct even if the animation is cancelled or never runs.
- Measure connector geometry once per transition (not per frame) and avoid layout thrash. Prefer one reusable indicator using transform/gradient or the Web Animations API. No React state updates per animation frame and no heavy blur filters.
- On narrow screens, keep the pulse within the layout. If the stepper wraps or scrolls and a traveling pulse would overflow, use a brief active-pill fade instead.
- Under prefers-reduced-motion: switch instantly or use a very short fade with no traveling pulse.
Acceptance: buttons feel responsive without bouncing. Stage changes fade rather than blink. The blue indicator flows along the connectors. A Stage 1 → Stage 5 jump completes quickly without activating or loading the intermediate stages.

